{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794545778744,"sessionID":"ses_e2cbe0f67ffeARtW3ynN5Ce39R","part":{"id":"prt_1d3438038001Vx8Xf78I5hXAHl","sessionID":"ses_e2cbe0f67ffeARtW3ynN5Ce39R","messageID":"msg_1d3437187001SkHJpk1etucNDn","type":"step-start","snapshot":"ae18d70b8329d6481dde0d8d1fd7c35189220601"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794545785958,"sessionID":"ses_e2cbe0f67ffeARtW3ynN5Ce39R","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3439b20001qS079kbjFRpwAx","sessionID":"ses_e2cbe0f67ffeARtW3ynN5Ce39R","messageID":"msg_1d3437187001SkHJpk1etucNDn","type":"tool","id":"call_9f3802926b634aa1a0b3a00b","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794545785632,"end":1794545785958}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794545785962,"sessionID":"ses_e2cbe0f67ffeARtW3ynN5Ce39R","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34399bc002CSDeoEICH7277o","sessionID":"ses_e2cbe0f67ffeARtW3ynN5Ce39R","messageID":"msg_1d3437187001SkHJpk1etucNDn","type":"tool","id":"call_4aa1d578930e4380a066b721","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794545785276,"end":1794545785962}}}}
